emma: Heads up - I'm prototyping a tiny package called @redwood-inference/fetch-adapter to make our fetch guidance copy-pasteable. Goals: pick a runtime-safe fetch, export a typed alias, and ship both cjs+esm entrypoints. Thoughts?

jorge: Love it. Key requirements: 1) zero global polyfill by default (no polyfill-global imports), 2) runtime detection with dynamic import of undici only in node, 3) types that don't reference undici specifics.

nina: For package layout I'd do: dist/esm/index.js, dist/cjs/index.js, types/index.d.ts. package.json exports should map import->esm require->cjs. Keep the runtime code tiny so bundlers can tree-shake. Example exports mapping I used elsewhere: 

```json
{"exports":{"import":"./dist/esm/index.js","require":"./dist/cjs/index.js","./package.json":"./package.json"},"main":"./dist/cjs/index.js","module":"./dist/esm/index.js"}
```

rachel: For the runtime chooser code, prefer feature detection over instanceof checks. Here's a tiny implementation I sketched that also avoids touching globalThis in edge builds:

```js
export async function getFetch({ preferGlobal = true } = {}) {
  // prefer the platform-provided fetch for edge-like runtimes
  if (preferGlobal && typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function' && typeof globalThis.Request === 'function') {
    return globalThis.fetch;
  }
  // try undici in node (dynamic import so bundlers don't pull it in for edge)
  try {
    const mod = await import('undici');
    if (typeof mod.fetch === 'function') return mod.fetch;
  } catch (e) {
    // no undici available
  }
  // fallback: throw with actionable message
  throw new Error('No fetch available. Pass a fetch implementation to ClientOptions or install undici for node.');
}
```

jorge: Nice. For types, export a minimal alias in index.d.ts: `export type BasicFetch = (info: RequestInfo, init?: RequestInit) => Promise<Response>;` and in README show examples for Edge, Node ESM, and Node CJS.

emma: We should also include a tiny helper for bundlers: `isFetchCompatible(obj)` that validates duck-typed shape (has .text/.json/.body with getReader optional) to avoid Vite/webpack shims causing hard-to-debug crashes.

nina: Docs needs a small matrix: environment -> recommended pattern. Eg: Edge (Next.js/Cloudflare) -> do not polyfill; pass globalThis.fetch or let adapter pick it. Node ESM -> adapter will dynamic import undici if needed. Node CJS -> require('undici').fetch or use the cjs entrypoint. Add troubleshooting FAQ entries: ERR_REQUIRE_ESM, Request type mismatch, streaming missing getReader.

rachel: On packaging: include a tiny test matrix in CI that runs three smoke targets: node18, node20 with undici absent (simulate older node), and a Cloudflare Workers runtime smoke that executes the ESM build. We had regressions when we only tested node.

emma: One UX thought: export a synchronous helper that prefers globalThis.fetch but doesn't import undici (so safe for bundlers), and an async helper that will load undici if required. Name them `getGlobalFetch()` and `getFetchAsync()` to avoid surprise dynamic imports during build.

jorge: Good call. For sample-apps, add three tiny snippets: Next.js Edge (TSX) showing `new Client({ fetch: globalThis.fetch })`, Vite+SPA showing `import { getFetchAsync } from '@redwood-inference/fetch-adapter'` and Node CJS example using require+undici. That will reduce support tickets.

nina: Also add a short paragraph in README about why global polyfills are harmful: constructor identity mismatches, streaming differences, and duplicate TypeScript types. Provide explicit removal steps for common tools: Vite plugin, webpack polyfills, and next.config disabling polyfill.

rachel: I'll draft the package, add the exports map, and a minimal README. Emma, can you add the sample-app snippets and CI job?

emma: Yup will do. Opening WIP PR in sample-apps and sdk.

